## value words: a value is one 64-bit word and the hot path never touches memory to read it.
##   ...1    integer of 63 bits, value = word >> 1. A value that needs the 64th bit lives in a [3][value] cell.
##   ...010  string of at most 7 bytes: bits 3..5 hold the length, bytes 1..7 the text, the rest is zero.
##   ...000  pointer to a cell, always aligned on 8 bytes.
## Every value has ONE form: an integer that fits 63 bits is never boxed and a string of at most 7 bytes is
## never a cell, so two words differ only if the values differ, unless both point to cells.
## A cell starts with one word. Odd: the low byte is the kind, 1 string [(len<<8)|1][bytes][NUL], 3 int, 5 list
## [(len<<8)|5][elements*], 7 rc, 9 arc, 11 weak, 13 promise. Even: the address of a static descriptor,
## [self][(arity<<8)|2][name] for a constructor and [self][(n<<8)|4][key*]... for a record. A descriptor's first
## word is its own address, so a descriptor is also the one cell of a constructor without fields.
